<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leicestershire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leicestershire is a county in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, centred on the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it lay in the territory of the Corieltauvi; it later formed part of Mercia and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, and emerged as a shire after the English reconquest of the 10th century. The county was long known for sheep farming, hosiery and footwear, and includes the battlefield of Bosworth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leicestershire.jpg" alt="Leicestershire" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Nugent · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loughborough_Carillon_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4228475.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Leicestershire is a ceremonial county in the East Midlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> to the north, Rutland to the east, Northamptonshire to the south-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a> to the south-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> to the west. It covers 2,156 square kilometres and is generally lowland, with small rolling hills. The River Soar bisects it from south to north, flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> and Loughborough before it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> at the county boundary. West of the river lies Charnwood Forest, an upland area that contains Bardon Hill, the county's highest point at 278 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county has prehistoric earthworks, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> was a Roman settlement. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> settled the region in the 6th century and it became part of the Kingdom of Mercia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 records four wapentakes in the territory: Guthlaxton, Framland, Goscote and Gartree. The county name is first recorded in 1087 as Lægrecastrescir. The wapentakes later became hundreds, with Goscote divided into West and East Goscote and Sparkenhoe added.</p><p>The county has had a relatively settled history, but it was the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth Field</a> in 1485, which established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">Tudor dynasty</a>'s position as monarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its farming was long based on livestock. Robert Bakewell of Dishley, near Loughborough (1725 to 1795), was a pioneer of selective breeding, and his Leicester Longwool sheep were prized by farmers across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><p>Knitwear, hosiery and footwear were the traditional industries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> and the county, and the sheep on the county's coat of arms recognises this. Manufacturing began with hand knitting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and was fully industrialised by the end of the 19th century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> the Leicestershire coalfield in the north and west was worked, and the Coalville mines operated from the 1820s until 1986. Bellfounding has been carried on at Loughborough since the 14th century; in 1881 John Taylor and Company cast Great Paul for St Paul's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the largest bell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, at the centre of the county, is by far its largest settlement. Other towns include Loughborough, Melton Mowbray, Hinckley, Market Harborough and Coalville. Local government is divided between the county council with seven district councils and the separate city council of Leicester. The county is known for Stilton and Red Leicester cheeses and for Melton Mowbray pork pies. Among its places of interest are the Bosworth battlefield, Bradgate Park, Belvoir Castle and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill fort at Burrough Hill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leicestershire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mercia, from the Old English Mierce, the 'border people', took shape among Anglian settlers in the upper Trent valley, around what later became Staffordshire, Derbyshire and <b>Leicestershire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Corieltauvi occupied <b>Leicestershire</b>, Lincolnshire, Nottinghamshire and parts of the surrounding counties in the late Iron Age; the older name Coritani was corrected after an inscription naming them was found.</blockquote><a href="/state/corieltauvi">Corieltauvi</a></li></ul></section>
